From 741a1f042172ff499fdec3571dd3e5a84ee7c050 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Hughes <ryan@heyoodle.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:24:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ACPI: processor_idle: let LPI states freeze the tick in
 suspend-to-idle

The LPI states get no ->enter_s2idle callback, so on ACPI arm64 systems
suspend-to-idle never finds a state to enter with the tick frozen and
falls back to plain idle. timekeeping_suspend() never runs, and
CLOCK_MONOTONIC keeps advancing for as long as the system sleeps.

systemd measures service watchdogs on CLOCK_MONOTONIC and relies on it
stopping in suspend. On the NVIDIA N1x (ASUS ProArt P14), any sleep
longer than three minutes made systemd kill systemd-logind,
systemd-journald and boltd for missed watchdog pings the moment it
thawed. The restarted logind dropped the graphical session, and
NetworkManager, put to sleep through the killed logind's
PrepareForSleep, never woke up again, leaving the laptop with no
network after resume.

FFH LPI entry (PSCI CPU_SUSPEND on arm64) returns with interrupts still
disabled and FFH states already carry CPUIDLE_FLAG_RCU_IDLE for the
context tracking it does itself, which is what enter_s2idle_proper()
expects. Use the same entry for ->enter_s2idle on those states. The
hardware state does not change: s2idle already picked the deepest state
by exit latency through cpuidle_find_deepest_state(), and
find_deepest_state() picks the same one now. Only the tick and
timekeeping are frozen on top of it.
---
 drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c b/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
index 42e0e08..b27971d 100644
--- a/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
@@ -1194,9 +1194,18 @@ static void acpi_processor_setup_lpi_states(struct acpi_processor *pr)
 		state->exit_latency = lpi->wake_latency;
 		state->target_residency = lpi->min_residency;
 		state->flags |= arch_get_idle_state_flags(lpi->arch_flags);
-		if (i != 0 && lpi->entry_method == ACPI_CSTATE_FFH)
-			state->flags |= CPUIDLE_FLAG_RCU_IDLE;
 		state->enter = acpi_idle_lpi_enter;
+		if (i != 0 && lpi->entry_method == ACPI_CSTATE_FFH) {
+			state->flags |= CPUIDLE_FLAG_RCU_IDLE;
+			/*
+			 * FFH entry returns with interrupts still disabled, so
+			 * the state can also be entered for suspend-to-idle
+			 * with the tick frozen. Without ->enter_s2idle, s2idle
+			 * keeps timekeeping running and CLOCK_MONOTONIC
+			 * advances for as long as the system sleeps.
+			 */
+			state->enter_s2idle = acpi_idle_lpi_enter;
+		}
 		drv->safe_state_index = i;
 	}
 
